<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Knighthood</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as knightly</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Knighthood refers to the tradition of an elite class of mounted, heavily armed warriors bound by status and service to a ruler. Its armoured cavalry influenced Byzantine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heavy-cavalry">heavy cavalry</a> and, through several channels of transmission, the medieval European knightly tradition. Elite horsemen of this kind were the backbone of Sasanian military power.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/knighthood.jpg" alt="Knighthood" width="320" height="472" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Meister des Codex Manesse · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Codex_Manesse_Hartmann_von_Aue.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The idea of knighthood may have been inspired by the ancient Greek hippeis and the Roman equites, the Roman Ordo Equestris being a class of mounted nobles. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> generally fielded masses of infantry with an elite, the comitatus, that rode to battle but dismounted to fight, as at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-tours">Battle of Tours</a> in 732.</p><p>In the Early Middle Ages any well-equipped horseman could be called a knight, or miles in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>. The first knights appeared under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> in the 8th century, as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> took to horseback in wide campaigns of conquest and, with the stirrup, increasingly fought as true cavalry. The word knight comes from the Old English cniht, meaning boy or servant, and its sense moved to household retainer.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>By the 10th century the institution was well established. Higher nobles granted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fiefs</a> in return for loyalty, protection and service, and supplied knights with lodging, food, armour, weapons, horses and money. A knight generally held his land by military tenure, usually 40 days of service a year. In the 12th century knighthood became a social rank, separating milites gregarii, non-noble cavalrymen, from milites nobiles, true knights, while the armoured cavalryman took the separate name of man-at-arms.</p><p>The knighting ceremony, the accolade, was usually held at a great feast such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a> or Easter. It involved a ritual bath, a prayer vigil through the night, an oath, and a blow with a sword on the shoulders. The first military orders, the Knights of the Holy Sepulchre and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-hospitaller">Knights Hospitaller</a>, were founded shortly after the First Crusade of 1099, followed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-templar">Knights Templars</a> in 1118, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a> in 1170 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Knights</a> in 1190. They began as monastic orders protecting pilgrims and grew powerful with the crusader states.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-middle-ages">Late Middle Ages</a> knighthood was associated with chivalry, a code for the courtly Christian warrior, spread by the Matter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matter-of-britain">Matter of Britain</a>, the cycles about <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>'s paladins and King Arthur's Round Table. Gunpowder weapons such as the culverin began to make armoured knights obsolete, though the titles remained, and Maximilian I is often called the last knight. Orders of knighthood continue today, among them the Order of the Garter and the Sovereign Military Order of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malta">Malta</a>, and a knighthood is granted by a head of state, monarch or prelate for meritorious service.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Knight” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Over the 12th century knighthood became a rank of the lesser nobility: boys of <b>knightly</b> families were trained in a lord’s household and dubbed knights in a ceremony, and the Church tried to restrain their violence through the Peace and Truce of God and to turn it towards…</blockquote><a href="/societies/feudal-europe">Social Hierarchy of Feudal Europe</a></li><li><blockquote>Like other baronies, it was held by a French <b>knightly</b> family owing military service and counsel to the prince and governing a Greek peasant population from a castle.</blockquote><a href="/state/barony-of-kalavryta">Barony of Kalavryta</a></li><li><blockquote>The duchy was an agrarian land of <b>knightly</b> estates and small towns such as Neubrandenburg, with its brick Gothic gates, Friedland and Woldegk.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-mecklenburg-stargard">Duchy of Mecklenburg-Stargard</a></li><li><blockquote>Its <b>knightly</b> culture produced Minnesang poets.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-swabia">Duchy of Swabia</a></li></ul></section>
